  [1mSection 2 — Error handling and edge cases[0m

  [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is the one part of this that fails in the real world, in three ways: a browser blocking site data can
  throw [38;5;153mSecurityError[39m on access to [38;5;153mwindow.sessionStorage[39m itself; Safari's private mode has historically thrown on
  writes; and the stored JSON can be corrupt because it's user-editable via devtools. There's a fourth, purely ours: a
  page that forgets the [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag hits [38;5;153mAppSession is not defined[39m.

  The rule I propose, one line: [1menvironment failures degrade, contract violations throw.[0m

  - [38;5;153mgetUser()[39m never throws. Storage unavailable, missing key, or unparseable JSON all return [38;5;153mnull[39m — "nobody is logged
    in" is the safe answer for every one. On corrupt JSON it also removes the key so the next call isn't fighting the
    same bad data.
  - [38;5;153msetUser()[39m never throws on [3mstorage[0m failure; it returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m and warns to console. Login succeeding while tracking
    fails is not a reason to fail the login, so the submit handler logs the warning and carries on.
  - [38;5;153msetUser()[39m [1mdoes[0m throw a [38;5;153mTypeError[39m on a missing or non-string [38;5;153muserId[39m/[38;5;153musername[39m. That's a caller bug, not an environment
    condition, and swallowing it would produce a store that silently holds [38;5;153mundefined[39m.
  - Access is wrapped per call rather than probed once at load, so storage becoming unavailable mid-session is handled
    the same as at startup.

  The missing-script-tag case I'd leave as the raw [38;5;153mReferenceError[39m — it's loud, immediate, and points at the right line.
  Defending against it means every caller guarding [38;5;153mtypeof AppSession[39m, which is worse.

  [1mSection 3 — Testing and tooling[0m

  The repo has none: no test runner, no linter, no formatter, no [38;5;153mscripts[39m block, no dependencies, and zero tests. Since
  the store is the first thing here with real logic and edge cases worth pinning down, I'd like to set up tests before
  writing it, and this is the cheapest moment to decide.

  [1mThe fork on tests:[0m [38;5;153msession.js[39m is pure logic over a storage interface, so it's unit-testable in Node with a fake
  [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m — [3mif[0m the file is requireable. That costs a two-line tail ([38;5;153mif (typeof module !== "undefined") [39m
  [38;5;153mmodule.exports = AppSession;[39m), which matches the CommonJS already in [38;5;153msrc/[39m. With that, [38;5;153mnode --test[39m covers every branch
  above — round-trip, empty store, corrupt JSON, throwing storage, bad arguments — with [1mzero dependencies installed[0m.
  Without it, verification is manual clicking in a browser, which won't reach the corrupt-JSON or throwing-storage paths
  at all.


